  <section id="um-methods" aria-labelledby="um-methods-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div>
        <span class="mgdb-eyebrow">The resource in detail</span>
        <h2 id="um-methods-title">Methods</h2>
      </div>
      <p>The genetics, the sequencing and the bioinformatics behind the collection, as described by the group that built it. This text dates from the 2011 methods document and describes how the resource was made; the counts elsewhere on this page are current.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mgdb-prose">
      <h3 class="mgdb-leaf-heading">Key features of the population</h3>
      <p>UniformMu is a maize population developed specifically for genetics research. Its defining feature is that genetic variation between individual UniformMu plants is limited to mutations caused by Robertson&rsquo;s <em>Mutator</em> transposons &#40;McCarty <em>et al.</em>, 2005&#41;. That is achieved by introgressing active <em>Mu</em> transposons into the genetically uniform W22 inbred strain.</p>
      <p>The consequence is two things at once. The uniformity of the plants and seeds gives an excellent basis for phenotype comparison &mdash; uniform wild type controls &mdash; and mutations of individual genes are caused by <em>Mu</em> insertions whose positions are known. Gene function can then be studied at the biochemical, metabolic and whole-plant levels as a consequence of a specific change in gene sequence.</p>
      <div class="um-feature-grid">
        <div class="mgdb-card">
          <h4>Uniform background</h4>
          <p>W22, with active <em>Mu</em> introgressed into it.</p>
          <figure class="um-figure-inline">
            <a class="um-zoom" href="/images/uniformmu/figure1.png"><img src="/images/uniformmu/figure1.png" alt="Figure 1: the crossing scheme introgressing active Mu transposons into the uniform W22 inbred background." width="445" height="187" loading="lazy" /></a>
            <figcaption>Figure 1. The uniform W22 background.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="mgdb-card">
          <h4>Genetic stability</h4>
          <p><em>Mu</em> activity is monitored with the <em>bronze</em> colour marker, so lines that have gone inactive can be identified rather than assumed.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mgdb-card">
          <h4>Mapped, heritable insertions</h4>
          <p>An average of about ten insertions per line where a visible phenotype is present, about five where it is not. Forty ancestral <em>Mu</em> elements in the W22 inbred genome are defined, so they can be excluded.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mgdb-card">
          <h4>A sustainable seed resource</h4>
          <p>Stocks come from sib-pollinated F<sub>3</sub> plants. Sib-pollination preserves deleterious mutations as heterozygotes rather than losing them.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="mgdb-leaf-heading">Field genetics</h3>
      <p>To isolate new mutations, plants heterozygous for a highly active <em>MuDR</em> &mdash; self-pollinated ears segregating 3:1 for densely spotted <em>bz1</em>-mum9 and stable bronze kernels &mdash; are backcrossed to the standard W22 ACR inbred strain &#40;McCarty <em>et al.</em>, 2005&#41;. The resulting F<sub>1</sub> plants are grown, self-pollinated and screened for stable <em>bz1</em> seed. Because the F<sub>1</sub> plants are heterozygous for <em>MuDR</em>, the self-pollinated ears segregate <em>Mu</em>-inactive seed with a stable bronze, non-spotted kernel phenotype. Those stable bronze F<sub>2</sub> seed are grown and self-pollinated to establish confirmed homozygous <em>MuDR</em>&#40;&minus;&#41;, <em>Mu</em>-inactive derivative lines.</p>
      <p>Twenty spot-free F<sub>3</sub> kernels are selected from each line, grown, and the plants sib-pollinated. A balanced pool of 200&ndash;300 seed is prepared from two to five ears per family, and every stock is screened manually to eliminate residual <em>Mu</em> activity, cracking, mould and other defects that affect viability. Plants are grown and pollinated at the UF PSREU experimental farm at Citra, Florida, and at the Gray Research facility in Askum, Illinois &mdash; Florida allows two generations a year, while the milder central Illinois summer is better for producing high-quality seed.</p>
      <figure class="um-figure-inline">
        <a class="um-zoom" href="/images/uniformmu/figure4.png"><img src="/images/uniformmu/figure4.png" alt="Figure 4: the field genetics scheme producing Mu-inactive F3 seed stocks for sequence indexing and public distribution." width="356" height="286" loading="lazy" /></a>
        <figcaption>Figure 4. Producing F<sub>3</sub> UniformMu seed stocks for sequence-indexing and public distribution.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3 class="mgdb-leaf-heading">Sequence-indexing by Illumina sequencing</h3>
      <p>Plants are grown in 24 &times; 24 grids &mdash; 576 lines per grid &mdash; creating a two-dimensional array of 48 pooled DNA samples, 24 for each axis. The 48 samples are sequenced in a single multiplexed library on one lane of a flow cell. Multiplexing uses a custom set of adaptor oligos, each carrying one of 64 four-base key codes at the 5&prime; end; the fourth base is a checksum that detects sequencing errors in the key. To eliminate chimeric PCR artefacts that cause key swapping between samples, every step of library construction is performed separately per sample, and sub-libraries are pooled only at the cluster generation stage.</p>
      <p>Each DNA sample is randomly sheared, end-polished, and ligated to a universal adaptor. PCR I uses a <em>Mu</em> TIR-specific primer and the universal adaptor primer; a nested PCR II incorporates the flow-cell adaptor primers into <em>Mu</em>-specific amplicons. The 3&prime; half of the long A-sequencing adaptor is fused to a nested TIR-specific oligo anchored six bases from the 3&prime; end of the TIR, and those six bases of TIR in the genomic read are what confirm an authentic <em>Mu</em>-flanking product &mdash; fewer than 1% of reads are rejected for mispriming. PCR II products are size-selected by excision from agarose gels, each sample on its own gel to rule out cross-contamination, and a third reaction completes the 5&prime; portion of the sequencing adaptor. Every PCR step uses the minimum number of cycles to limit bias.</p>
      <figure class="um-figure-inline um-figure-wide">
        <a class="um-zoom" href="/images/uniformmu/figure5.png"><img src="/images/uniformmu/figure5.png" alt="Figure 5: the library construction scheme, showing the nested TIR primers, the universal adaptor, and the anchored genomic sequence retained in each read." width="678" height="311" loading="lazy" /></a>
        <figcaption>Figure 5. Library construction and the anchored <em>Mu</em>-flanking read.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3 class="mgdb-leaf-heading">Mapping the flanking sequences</h3>
      <p>A 72-base run yields 25&ndash;32 million reads per lane, giving more than 500,000 quality-trimmed reads per multiplexed sample. After the key code is parsed and the TIR sequence trimmed, reads retain 38 bases of precisely anchored genomic sequence flanking the insertion. Trimmed reads are mapped with BLASTN run in parallel; the output is parsed into a database of insertion sites at an expectation cutoff of 10<sup>&minus;7</sup>, and flanking sequences mapping within 3 bp of each other are assigned to the same insertion locus.</p>
      <p>For each locus, read counts in each grid axis locate the lines carrying that insertion. Loci that map to low-copy locations &mdash; a maximum of four unresolved copies, with over 95% single copy &mdash; and that have unambiguous, statistically robust axis assignments are given a unique identifier such as <code>mu103480</code> and submitted to MaizeGDB. In parallel, seed stocks of at least 200 high-quality seed per line are deposited at the Stock Center. Consensus flanking sequences are assembled from the reads and the paired flanking sequences deposited in GenBank as gapped 76 bp sequences.</p>
      <figure class="um-figure-inline um-figure-wide">
        <a class="um-zoom" href="/images/uniformmu/figure6.png"><img src="/images/uniformmu/figure6.png" alt="Figure 6: the grid design, showing how read counts on each axis of the 24 by 24 array identify which line carries an insertion." width="703" height="431" loading="lazy" /></a>
        <figcaption>Figure 6. Locating a line in the grid from axis read counts.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3 class="mgdb-leaf-heading">Historical figures</h3>
      <p>These two figures are from the March 2011 data release, when the collection held 26,211 insertions in 5,127 stocks. They are kept because published work refers to them; the current numbers are in the coverage section above.</p>
      <div class="um-figure-pair">
        <figure class="um-figure-inline">
          <a class="um-zoom" href="/images/uniformmu/table1.png"><img src="/images/uniformmu/table1.png" alt="Table 1: the March 2011 release summary, giving insertion, stock and gene counts." width="316" height="416" loading="lazy" /></a>
          <figcaption>Table 1. The March 2011 release summary.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="um-figure-inline">
          <a class="um-zoom" href="/images/uniformmu/figure2.png"><img src="/images/uniformmu/figure2.png" alt="Figure 2: the distribution of UniformMu insertions across the maize genome as of the 2011 release." width="229" height="318" loading="lazy" /></a>
          <figcaption>Figure 2. Insertion distribution, 2011 release.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

